  "metadata": {
    "name": "toplevel_ref_metaparameter_axis",
    "description": "Conformance pin for the \u00a74.7 edge pipeline at a top-level models.<k> {ref}. Mounts toplevel_ref_metaparameter_axis_lib.esm, whose only axis is sized by the LEAF's own metaparameter (NLEV, default 4). \u00a74.7 \"Two mount forms, one mechanism\" forbids the two attachment points from differing, and the edge pipeline's step (1) resolves the referenced document in its OWN scope, so the leaf's metaparameters close and fold AT THE MOUNT (\u00a79.7.6 site 3) and `lev` reaches the importing registry as size 4 \u2014 the importer redeclares nothing, which is \u00a79.7.5's \"without redeclaring them\". Python and Rust do this. Julia still inlines the top-level form with a raw pre-pass that defers \u00a79.7 to the root document, so it holds the unfolded declaration back and loads with `lev` undeclared; that remaining divergence is recorded in ESM_COMPLIANCE_VALIDATION_MATRIX.md BEHAV-04-D-003 and is why this pair lives under tests/fixtures/ rather than tests/valid/ (TypeScript and Go do not mount a top-level {ref} at all).",
    "authors": ["EarthSciAST"],
    "created": "2026-09-07T00:00:00Z",
    "tags": ["subsystem", "index_sets", "metaparameter", "mount", "toplevel-ref", "regression"]
  },
